WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 224: Austria: Maria Theresa, the Pragmatic Sanction, and the Schönbrunn Palace

1
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.160
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:21.760
[Rebecca] Today, we journey into the majestic halls of Vienna and the sprawling lands of the Habsburg monarchy to follow the life of a 23-year-old woman who inherited an empire on the brink of dismemberment and transformed it into a powerhouse of European culture.

3
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:26.560
[Rebecca] Empress Maria Teresa.

4
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.560
[George] In October 1740, Holy Roman Emperor Charles V.

5
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.840
[George] I died unexpectedly after eating poisoned mushrooms, leaving no male heir.

6
00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:51.520
[George] For decades, Charles had spent his fortune and diplomatic leverage securing European treaties recognizing the pragmatic sanction, an imperial decree declaring that Habsburg lands were indivisible and could be inherited by his eldest daughter, Maria Teresa.

7
00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:58.720
[George] But the moment the emperor closed his eyes, European monarchs treated the signed treaties as worthless scraps of paper.

8
00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:13.280
[George] Maria Teresa was just 23 years old, heavily pregnant, with no administrative experience, an empty treasury containing less than 100,000 florins, and a demoralized army whose soldiers had not been paid in months.

9
00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.880
[George] The vultured dynasties of Europe smelled an easy kill.

10
00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:26.080
[George] France, Spain, Bavaria, and Saxony prepared to carve up the Habsburg territories, while from the north came the swiftest and most treacherous blow.

11
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:40.720
[George] Charles V.

12
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:48.160
[George] I had extracted signed guarantees for the pragmatic sanction from Britain, France, Russia, Spain, and Prussia.

13
00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:57.600
[George] Yet Prince Eugene of Savoy prophetically warned the Emperor that a full treasury and an army of 200,000 men were worth more than all the treaties in Europe.

14
00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:11.720
[George] Maria Theresa's husband, Francis Stephen of Lorraine, was a genial and handsome prince who had sacrificed his ancestral Duchy of Lorraine to marry her, devoting himself to financial investments that made him one of the wealthiest men in Europe.

15
00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:18.760
[George] With Prussian, French, and Bavarian armies advancing on Vienna, Maria Theresa made an audacious gamble.

16
00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:29.320
[George] She travelled to Pressburg, present-day Bratislava, to appeal directly to the fierce and independent Hungarian nobility, who had historically rebelled against Habsburg rule.

17
00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:34.600
[George] On September 11, 1741, dressed in deep mourning black with St.

18
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:42.200
[George] Stephen's ancient crown upon her head, the twenty four-year-old monarch addressed the assembled Hungarian diet in fluent Latin.

19
00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:53.160
[George] With tears in her eyes and holding her infant son, she declared that she was abandoned by all the world and placed her faith entirely in the ancient loyalty and valor of the Hungarian nation.

20
00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.160
[George] The effect was electrifying.

21
00:02:55.160 --> 00:03:02.680
[George] The Hungarian magnates drew their sabres from their scabbards, raising them aloft in the hall and shouting with thunderous roar.

22
00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:06.760
[George] Vitem et sanguinum pro rige nostro Maria Teresa.

23
00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:10.040
[George] Our lives and our blood for our King Maria Teresa.

24
00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:16.840
[George] Hungary mobilized sixty thousand fierce Hazar horsemen who fell upon the invading French and Bavarian forces.

25
00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:22.040
[George] Driving them from Bohemia and saving the Habsburg dynasty from complete destruction.

26
00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:36.040
[George] The Hungarian nobles had spent centuries resisting Habsburg's central authority, yet Maria Teresa's emotional plea and feminine dignity disarmed their historical grievances, forging a personal loyalty that endured for centuries.

27
00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:50.400
[George] The Hungarian hussars who galloped to Maria Theresa's defense were legendary light horsemen whose lightning cavalry raids, curved sabres, and brightly braided dolmen jackets struck terror into the ranks of French and Bavarian foot soldiers.

28
00:03:50.400 --> 00:04:01.680
[George] Although the War of the Austrian Succession ended with the bitter loss of Silesia to Frederick the Great, Maria Theresa emerged from the conflict with iron determination to never be caught weak again.

29
00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:11.040
[George] She recognized that the medieval patchwork of Habsburg territories could only survive through rigorous administrative centralization and economic modernization.

30
00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:26.880
[George] Aided by her brilliant chancellor, Count Friedrich Wilhelm von Hogwitz, she stripped local noble assemblies of their tax monopolies, establishing a permanent state bureaucracy and forcing the wealthy nobility and Catholic Church to pay taxes for the first time.

31
00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:43.680
[George] She founded the Therzian Military Academy at Wiener Neustadt to train professional army officers based on ability rather than birth, instituted compulsory primary education for all children aged 6 to 12 across Austria, and outlawed which burning and judicial torture.

32
00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:55.280
[George] Vienna became a flourishing administrative capital, its streets paved, its public health monitored by royal physicians, and its imperial finances restored to unprecedented stability.

33
00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:10.080
[George] Count Hogwitz's fiscal reforms doubled Austrian state revenues within five years, creating a standardized taxation system that eliminated hereditary feudal exemptions and funded a permanent standing army of 100,000 soldiers.

34
00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:23.520
[George] The Empress's educational reforms established state funded schools in every Austrian village, introducing standardized textbooks and teacher training that raised literacy rates across the empire to among the highest in Europe.

35
00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:40.360
[George] To reclaim the lost province of Silesia and isolate Prussia, Maria Theresa's brilliant foreign minister, Count Wenzel Anton von Conitz, engineered the most astonishing diplomatic realignment in European history, the diplomatic revolution of 1756.

36
00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:50.840
[George] For two and a half centuries, the Habsburgs and the French Kingdom of the Bourbons had been bitter mortal enemies, fighting countless devastating wars across Italy and Germany.

37
00:05:50.840 --> 00:06:00.600
[George] Conitz boldly proposed that Austria and France bury their ancient enmity and unite in a grand defensive alliance, joined by Empress Elizabeth of Russia.

38
00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:11.400
[George] Frederick the Great found himself surrounded by a coalition of three great continental empresses, Maria Theresa, Elizabeth of Russia, and Madame de Pompadour of France.

39
00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:30.120
[George] When the Seven Years' War erupted, Austrian forces inflicted humiliating defeats on Frederick's armies at Collin and Hochkirch, proving that Maria Theresa's military reforms had turned Austria into a world-class fighting machine, cementing Austria's standing as the supreme Catholic power of Central Europe.

40
00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:44.040
[George] The alliance between the Habsburgs and the Bourbons was sealed by a treaty signed at Versailles in May 1756, astonishing European diplomats who had regarded the two royal houses as irreconcilable rivals for over two hundred years.

41
00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:57.400
[George] The Seven Years' War showcased the brilliant generalship of Austrian Field Marshal Leopold von Don, whose disciplined defensive tactics broke Frederick the Great's vaunted army at the Battle of Kahlen in 1757.

42
00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:06.920
[George] Outside her state duties, Maria Theresa was a deeply affectionate mother who bore sixteen children with her beloved husband, Francis Stephen of Lorraine.

43
00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:17.280
[George] She transformed the imperial summer residence of Schoenbrunn Palace into a dazzling rococo masterpiece of golden galleries, mirrors, and manicured Baroque gardens.

44
00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:27.520
[George] There, in the palace's grand rooms, a six-year-old musical prodigy named Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart leaped into the Empress's lap and planted a kiss on her cheek.

45
00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:32.320
[George] Yet beneath her maternal warmth lay cold Habsburg dynastic calculation.

46
00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:43.440
[George] Maria Theresa used her children as diplomatic chess pieces, marrying her daughters into the royal houses of France, Spain, Naples and Parma to seal strategic alliances.

47
00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:54.240
[George] Her youngest and most beautiful daughter, Maria Antonia, known to history as Marie Antoinette, was sent to Versailles at the age of fourteen to marry the Dauphin of France.

48
00:07:54.240 --> 00:08:00.960
[George] A fateful union designed to guarantee European peace that would later end beneath the blade of the French guillotine.

49
00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:16.160
[George] Schoenbrunn Palace became a vibrant family estate echoing with the laughter of sixteen royal children, where Maria Theresa took personal charge of their daily schedules, religious instruction, and language lessons in German, French, and Italian.

50
00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:30.160
[George] The gardens of Schoenbrunn were designed with grand symmetrical parterres, marble fountains depicting Neptune and sea nymphs, and a towering neoclassical Gloriet perched upon the hill, offering panoramic views over the city of Vienna.

51
00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:42.640
[George] When her husband Francis died suddenly in 1765, Maria Theresa was shattered by grief, dressing in black mourning attire for the remaining fifteen years of her life and cutting her hair short.

52
00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:49.920
[George] She elevated her eldest son, Joseph I I, as Holy Roman Emperor and co-regent of the Habsburg lands.

53
00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:59.200
[George] The partnership between mother and son was fraught with intense ideological friction, while Maria Teresa was a deeply devout Catholic who believed in cautious.

54
00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:13.480
[George] Pragmatic reform anchored in tradition, young Joseph was an impatient radical disciple of the Enlightenment who wanted to abolish feudalism overnight, suppress monastic orders, and impose sweeping secularism by imperial decree.

55
00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:24.680
[George] Maria Teresa repeatedly restrained her son's reckless impulses, warning him that hasty reforms imposed without the consent of the people would breed resentment and tear the empire apart.

56
00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:34.600
[George] Despite their arguments, their combined leadership propelled Austria into the modern era with an unparalleled wave of civic, legal, and educational reforms.

57
00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:35.480
[George] Joseph I.

58
00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:53.240
[George] Maria Theresa and Joseph I.

59
00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:57.000
[George] maintained a deeply affectionate yet turbulent relationship.

60
00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:05.720
[George] The Empress frequently wept over her son's blunt cynicism, while Joseph complained that his mother's caution prevented Austria from achieving greatness.

61
00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:14.920
[George] Maria Teresa passed away in Vienna on November 29, 1780, at the age of 63, surrounded by her children and ministers.

62
00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:20.360
[George] Her bitter rival, Frederick the Great, paid her the highest tribute when he learned of her death.

63
00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:23.320
[George] She brought honor to her throne and to her sex.

64
00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:26.840
[George] I made war upon her, but I was never her enemy.

65
00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:34.600
[George] Maria Teresa had inherited an empire on the brink of total collapse, bankrupt, divided, and invaded on all sides.

66
00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:46.880
[George] Through forty years of unyielding courage, administrative genius, and moral authority, she had not only preserved the Habsburg monarchy, but remade it into a unified, modern European power.

67
00:10:44.600 --> 00:11:02.880
[George] She laid the social and cultural foundations that made Vienna the undisputed musical, artistic, and intellectual capital of 19th century Europe, leaving behind a legacy of resilience and enlightened leadership that remains the golden age of Austrian history.

68
00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:09.200
[George] Maria Theresa's mortal illness in November 1780 was met with quiet Christian resignation.

69
00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:14.960
[George] When her son Joseph urged her to sleep, she replied, I am about to meet my creator.

70
00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:16.880
[George] I wish to meet him awake.

71
00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:30.720
[George] Maria Theresa was laid to rest in the imperial crypt of the Capuchin Church in Vienna, in a magnificent double sarcophagus adorned with lifelike bronze statues of herself and her beloved husband Francis gazing toward heaven.

72
00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:56.640
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

